<file path=".github/workflows/security.yml">
name: Security

# Secret scanning with gitleaks. CodeQL and dependency-review are left out on
# purpose: they need GitHub Advanced Security on private repositories, which
# this repo does not have.

on:
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  schedule:
    - cron: "17 5 * * 1" # Mondays 05:17 UTC
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read

concurrency:
  group: security-${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

env:
  # Update both together: version and the sha256 of its linux_x64 archive from
  # https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases (gitleaks_<version>_checksums.txt).
  GITLEAKS_VERSION: "8.30.1"
  GITLEAKS_SHA256: "551f6fc83ea457d62a0d98237cbad105af8d557003051f41f3e7ca7b3f2470eb"

jobs:
  gitleaks:
    name: gitleaks
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 10
    steps:
      - name: Checkout (full history)
        uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
          persist-credentials: false

      # The binary is pinned and checksum-verified instead of using a third-party
      # action (the official action needs a paid licence for organisation repos).
      - name: Install gitleaks
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          archive="gitleaks_${GITLEAKS_VERSION}_linux_x64.tar.gz"
          curl -fsSL -o "$RUNNER_TEMP/$archive" \
            "https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${GITLEAKS_VERSION}/${archive}"
          echo "${GITLEAKS_SHA256}  $RUNNER_TEMP/$archive" | sha256sum --check --strict
          mkdir -p "$RUNNER_TEMP/gitleaks"
          tar -xzf "$RUNNER_TEMP/$archive" -C "$RUNNER_TEMP/gitleaks" gitleaks
          echo "$RUNNER_TEMP/gitleaks" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"

      # Scans every commit reachable from the checked-out ref. --redact keeps
      # matched values out of the CI log. A finding exits non-zero and fails the check.
      - name: Scan git history for secrets
        run: gitleaks git --redact --no-banner --verbose .
</file>

<file path=".github/dependabot.yml">
version: 2

updates:
  - package-ecosystem: npm
    directory: /
    schedule:
      interval: weekly
      day: monday
      time: "06:00"
      timezone: Europe/Rome
    open-pull-requests-limit: 5
    labels: [dependencies]
    commit-message:
      prefix: "chore(deps)"
    groups:
      # Minor and patch updates arrive as one PR; majors stay separate for review.
      npm-minor-patch:
        update-types: [minor, patch]

  # Actions are pinned to commit SHAs; Dependabot bumps the SHA and the version comment.
  - package-ecosystem: github-actions
    directory: /
    schedule:
      interval: weekly
      day: monday
      time: "06:00"
      timezone: Europe/Rome
    open-pull-requests-limit: 5
    labels: [dependencies, ci]
    commit-message:
      prefix: "ci(deps)"
    groups:
      github-actions:
        patterns: ["*"]
</file>

<file path="content/pages/en/about.mdx">
---
{
  "heading": "About: AI and full-stack engineer",
  "lead": "I am an independent AI and full-stack engineer based in Turin. I design and build agents, automation and web applications with the people who need them, from idea to release."
}
---

## What I do

I work on four things: autonomous AI agents, process automation, production web applications and document intelligence. I combine them because real problems need them together: a useful agent needs reliable data, a clear interface and infrastructure that holds up. I work with startups, SMEs and enterprises in Italy and across the European Union.

## How I work

I work directly with you, with no middle layer: the person who listens to the problem is the one who designs and writes the code. I would rather start from a prototype evaluated on real cases than from a long specification, and I say right away when AI is not the right answer. The [way I work](/process) describes the phases one by one.

## What I care about

Quality you can verify, with automated tests and checks. Transparency about what the AI does and where a person steps in. Care for privacy and security from the design stage: collecting as little data as possible and keeping secrets away from the browser. Accessibility: what I build has to be usable by everyone.

## Where I am

I work from Turin, in Italian and in English. If you want to know whether I can help, [get in touch](/contact).
</file>

<file path="content/pages/it/about.mdx">
---
{
  "heading": "Chi sono: ingegnere AI e full-stack",
  "lead": "Sono un ingegnere AI e full-stack indipendente a Torino. Progetto e realizzo agenti, automazioni e applicazioni web insieme a chi ne ha bisogno, dall'idea al rilascio."
}
---

## Il mio lavoro

Mi occupo di quattro cose: agenti AI autonomi, automazione dei processi, applicazioni web in produzione e document intelligence. Le unisco perché i problemi reali le richiedono insieme: un agente utile ha bisogno di dati affidabili, di un'interfaccia chiara e di un'infrastruttura che regga. Mi rivolgo a startup, PMI e grandi aziende in Italia e nell'Unione europea.

## Come lavoro

Lavoro direttamente con te, senza intermediari: chi ascolta il problema è anche chi progetta e scrive il codice. Preferisco partire da un prototipo valutato su casi reali piuttosto che da una lunga specifica, e dico subito quando l'AI non è la risposta giusta. Il [metodo di lavoro](/process) descrive le fasi una per una.

## Che cosa mi sta a cuore

La qualità che si può verificare, con test e controlli automatici. La trasparenza su che cosa fa l'AI e dove interviene una persona. L'attenzione alla privacy e alla sicurezza fin dalla progettazione: raccogliere meno dati possibile e tenere i segreti lontano dal browser. L'accessibilità: ciò che costruisco deve poter essere usato da tutti.

## Dove sono

Lavoro da Torino, in italiano e in inglese. Se vuoi capire se posso aiutarti, [scrivimi](/contact).
</file>

<file path="content/services/en/ai-agents.mdx">
---
{
  "title": "Autonomous AI agents",
  "summary": "Agents that use your tools to do real work, with clear controls and a person who decides at the critical steps.",
  "seoTitle": "Custom autonomous AI agents",
  "description": "I build AI agents that qualify leads, route requests and answer from your documents, with evaluation, traceability and a person in the loop.",
  "order": 1,
  "idealFor": [
    "Teams handling many repetitive requests: email, tickets, messages",
    "Companies whose internal knowledge is scattered across documents and systems",
    "Startups that want an assistant inside their own product"
  ],
  "outcomes": [
    "Less time on repetitive work and more on the cases that matter",
    "Traceable answers and decisions, with the source named",
    "A clear line between what the agent does alone and what a person approves"
  ],
  "deliverables": [
    "A working agent, integrated with the tools you already use",
    "A set of test cases to evaluate its quality over time",
    "An action log to check what the agent did",
    "Documentation for use and maintenance"
  ],
  "stack": ["TypeScript", "Next.js", "Claude API", "PostgreSQL"],
  "demo": "instagram-lead-agent"
}
---

## Where it works well

Agents pay off where work is made of many similar steps and decisions you can describe: qualifying a lead, routing a request to the right team, drafting a reply, finding and summarising information spread across several sources.

## How I build it

I start from one specific task and real examples. The agent gets only the tools and data it needs, with limited permissions. Before release I evaluate it on a set of test cases, and afterwards I track its quality and cost, because an agent needs the same watching as any other system.

## Controls and limits

At critical steps the agent prepares and a person approves, and every action is recorded. Integrations with platforms such as Instagram or LinkedIn go through their official APIs only, within their terms of use: I do not automate personal accounts or work around platform limits. If you want to know whether an agent fits your case, [let's talk](/contact).
</file>

