---
{
  "title": "Document intelligence",
  "summary": "Extraction, classification and search over documents, with checked results and human review of uncertain cases.",
  "seoTitle": "Document intelligence for businesses",
  "description": "Systems that read contracts, invoices and technical documents: they extract data, classify and make them searchable, with checks and human review where needed.",
  "order": 4,
  "idealFor": [
    "Firms and companies with many documents to read, compare and file",
    "Back offices that key in data from invoices and forms by hand",
    "Teams that need to find answers in unstructured archives"
  ],
  "outcomes": [
    "Extracted data in a structured format, ready for your systems",
    "Every answer with a pointer to the passage it came from",
    "Human review focused only on the doubtful cases"
  ],
  "deliverables": [
    "A reading and extraction flow configured on your documents",
    "An accuracy evaluation on a real sample",
    "A review interface for uncertain cases",
    "Documentation, including how personal data is handled"
  ],
  "stack": ["TypeScript", "Claude API", "PostgreSQL"],
  "demo": "document-intelligence-demo"
}
---

## From piles of documents to usable data

Contracts, invoices, data sheets, forms: the information is there, but locked in files that are hard to search and compare. I build systems that read documents, extract the fields you need, classify them and let you search in plain language.

## Every result can be checked

Answers point to the passage of the document they come from. Extracted data is checked against defined rules and formats, and uncertain cases go to a person through a review interface. Before release I measure accuracy on a sample of your own documents, so you know what to expect.

## Where the data goes

Documents may contain personal or confidential data. Together we define where they are processed, what is kept and for how long, according to the type of data and the requirements that apply to your case. To start, [tell me](/contact) what documents you have and what you would like to get out of them.
</file>

